North Korea is looking to capture the  world's attention as it puts on what can  be described as a parade for the ages to  celebrate its 70th anniversary just  months after summoning reporters to  witness the destruction of a nuclear  test site the country is looking to make  another powerful statement Ben Tracy is  in Pyongyang the North Korean capital  this morning  Ben good morning north Korea has invited  more than 100 journalists and thousands  of tourists from all over the world to  come here to Pyongyang this weekend for  these major events that it plans to put  on to celebrate its founding day now  that does include a major military  parade through the streets of Pyongyang  the big question is whether Kim jong-un  will show off the intercontinental  ballistic missiles that North Korea  claims are capable of hitting the United  States  he says he's serious about  denuclearization and has recently tried  to appear less confrontational  so not parading the ICBMs may be a sign  of goodwill now since we arrived here  the North Koreans have wanted to show us  another side of their country taking us  to a teaching College where young women  are being trained as elementary school  teachers and also to a cosmetics factory  in recent months Kim Jong moon has  really stressed to his people that he  wants to focus on education and growing  North Korea's economy now the reality of  course is that North Korea still has all  of its nuclear weapons and there has  been little progress in trying to  convince Kim jong-eun to give them up  for CBS this morning Saturday Ben Tracy  Pyongyang North Korea
 

